{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-apocalypse-aa/1519853-building-a-better-hauglosk","id":1519853,"title":"Building a better Hauglosk","path":["Home","Onyx Path","White Wolf Worlds","World of Darkness","Werewolf: The Apocalypse"],"posts":[{"date":"2023-08-05T05:41:06-07:00","id":1519853,"title":"Building a better Hauglosk","content":"Despite my criticism of W5, I don't actually mind the idea of an opposite to Harano. What I mind is this opposite being a tool to punish players and explain why a tribe cannot be played.<br /> <br /> So, I figured let's make use of our creativity and think of a better Hauglosk that can be fun to play.<br /> <br /> Some personal ground rules;<br /> <br /> -Like Harano, Hauglosk is a mental disorder. It should not be treated as a punishment but as a way to enhance play.<br /> <br /> - Like with Harano, a whole tribe cannot be under it as it is a personal thing.<br /> <br /> - If Harano is depression and a feeling of hopelessness at thing not yet lost, Hauglosk should be equally about a false reality.<br /> <br /> <br /> <br /> My ideas; Hauglosk is the feeling of euphoria and restlessness. It isn't that you think everything is always great, but you just don't dwell on it too much. You want to do stuff NOW, not wait for a year. So you challenge for rank NOW, you try to create that fetish NOW and despite some successes, you aren't really considering your actions. Your short-term memory is affected and you get intrusive thoughts that at times run contrary to your own morality. You impulse buy, you drink to excess and you burn yourself out.<br /> <br /> This mania might seem better than Harano, but Hauglosk makes regaining Rage harder. You just aren't tapping into the primal anger and instead you go with the flow. Red Talons call Hauglosk wolf-heart with dog-mind, as the affected garou often acts closer to a regular wolf with little concept of future and past. Only made worse.<br /> <br /> This is a VERY rough draft, but I just wanted to get the ball rolling. My idea is that Hauglosk is a combination of bi-polar mania and OCDs intrusive thoughts with the added supernatural effect that the character leans too closely on their animal side's mindset.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><a href=\"https://ana-mizuki.deviantart.com/\" target=\"_blank\">My gallery.</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":4}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/110-ana-mizuki","name":"Ana Mizuki","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar110_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2484"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-08-05T06:24:26-07:00","id":1519856,"title":"","content":"<br /> Lifting from a certain game's Satiety mechanics...<br /> <br /> <br /> <b>Rage</b> is a gauge measured from 0 to 10. Gained and lost not as fast as typical of 'magic points' (since they aren't in this form), but still expected to fluctuate dynamically.<br /> <br /> There are five Statuses spread across Rage.<br /> <br /> <br /> <b>Harano (Rage 0)</b>: Your Rage has been extinguished. The end of the world is nigh, and there is <b>nothing</b> you can do about it.<br /> <br /> <i>(Something about failing all Social actions that are not about projecting your own despair at others, in addition to Chilled below.)</i><br /> <br /> <br /> <b>Chilled (Rage 1-3)</b>: The Rage inside you wanes, dries up, and cools. Your spirit languishes, sapping strength from your limbs, but opening your mind up to alternate avenues.<br /> <br /> <i>(Something like bonuses to non-destructive and non-confrontational Mental actions, at the expense of penalties to Physical or destructive actions.)</i><br /> <br /> <br /> <b>Tepid (Rage 4-6)</b>: The Rage inside you has reached a temperate point. Your spirit is balanced between drive and despair, but this confers no distinct power.<br /> <br /> <i>(Something about Losing the Wolf.)</i><br /> <br /> <br /> <b>Inflamed (Rage 7-9)</b>: The Rage inside you waxes, spills over, and burns. Your spirit frets, urging you to flex your muscles, but leaves you glossing over the nuances of life.<br /> <br /> <i>(Something like bonuses to Physical or destructive actions, at the expense of penalties to non-destructive and non-confrontational Mental actions)</i><br /> <br /> <br /> <b>Hauglosk (Rage 10)</b>: Your Rage consumes you. The end of the world is nigh, and you will do something. <b>Anything</b>.<br /> <br /> <i>(Something about failing all Social actions that are not about projecting your own drive at others, in addition to Inflamed above.)</i><br /> <br /> \u200b","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>MtAw Homebrew:<br /> <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/1051456-even-more-legacies-updated-to-2e#post1051456\" target=\"_blank\">Even more Legacies, updated to 2E</a><br /> <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/1443819-new-2e-legacies-expanded\" target=\"_blank\">New 2E Legacies, expanded</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12619-21c-hermit","name":"21C Hermit","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12619_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Oct 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3489"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-08-05T07:38:13-07:00","id":1519859,"title":"","content":"Hauglosk and Harano sound a lot like the two facets of bipolar disorder (mania and depression).","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Amethyst is my birthstone. She/they.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/26996-amethyst","name":"Amethyst","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar26996_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Aug 2021"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"873"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-08-05T07:48:36-07:00","id":1519862,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Amethyst</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-apocalypse-aa/1519853-building-a-better-hauglosk?p=1519859#post1519859\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Hauglosk and Harano sound a lot like the two facets of bipolar disorder (mania and depression).</div> </div> </div> </div>That's what I'm going for. With the added tie that Harano is more human (loss in the abstract, overthinking) while Hauglosk is more animal (short-term planning, impulsivity.)<br /> <br /> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12619-21c-hermit\" style=\"background-image:url('https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5//avatar12619_2.png');\" class=\"b-bbcode-user b-bbcode-user--has-avatar js-bbcode-user\" data-userid=\"12619\" data-vbnamecard=\"12619\">21C Hermit</a> I wouldn't tie Hauglosk to Rage, because Harano isn't either. Hauglosk is making recovering Rage hard, because the garou is no longer tapping to the well of anger because they are too busy in their mania.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><a href=\"https://ana-mizuki.deviantart.com/\" target=\"_blank\">My gallery.</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/110-ana-mizuki","name":"Ana Mizuki","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar110_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2484"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-08-05T22:59:12-07:00","id":1519934,"title":"","content":"My recommendation would be to build something based upon a better system of Rage dice, with the starting point being a system like that I have previously proposed (quoted here for reference).<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>James_Willoughby</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-apocalypse-aa/1516465-rage-dice-w5-preview?p=1517035#post1517035\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">What strikes me about Rage and Rage dice is that these seem to solely exist to punish players. Were I writing a new edition of <i>Werewolf: the Apocalypse</i>, I would have introduced something like the following:<ul><li>Rage is a trait representing the primal power of werewolves, and their connection to their animalistic nature.</li> <li>Rage is gained in various contexts but, seriously, we are going to give some proper, details examples, rather than just a bit of vague guidance.</li> <li>Each point of Rage a character has provides one Rage dice. You can choose to add Rage dice to a dice pool, which represents werewolves calling upon their primal energies. Adding these dice increase the chance of success on a given roll, but also present the chance of a brutal outcome; unlike the current version of the rules, brutal outcomes are not necessarily failures, but normally present downsides.</li> <li>An example: a Glass Walker corporate spy is attempting to sneak into an office building. The character doesn't have any suitable gifts, and, for various reasons, the character is not able to use the Umbra as a shortcut. Does the character add any Rage dice to the roll? These would increase the chance of success, but also increase the chance of a brutal outcome (maybe the character would lose patience trying to pick the lock, and just batter the door down instead).</li> <li>So, players have meaningful choices; do I draw on my Rage to increase the chance of success on a given roll, but with the chance of a brutal outcome?</li> <li>We want some guidance to brutal outcomes, beyond vague hand-waving, and brutal outcomes when rolls are successes can be as interesting as when they are failures. Remember the classic werewolf trope of victims being killed in a way to suggest that they have been attacked by a wolf or monster? That's going to happen if you defeat an enemy with a brutal outcome. It may of course, be worth it.</li> <li>And then sometimes a brutal outcome won't matter at all; trying to defeat a nexus crawler? Throw all the Rage dice you have at it, you're going to need it, and there won't be any consequences for ripping off a tentacle or two with your teeth.</li> <li>If we want to have penalties for having Rage which is too high, have a mechanic forcing Rage dice into a dice pool for every point by which Rage exceeds Willpower. They are still bonus dice, mind.</li> <li>And taking actions to reduce Rage are perfectly legitimate; we don't want sidebars telling players they shouldn't take actions just to reduce Rage. There's nothing wrong with characters being aware of their Rage, and taking actions to moderate it.</li> </ul><br /> So, the above is not significantly different from Rage as written, but with some subtle differences to make Rage dice a benefit, rather than a penalty, and giving players a bit more agency to how Rage intersects with actions of a given character.<br /></div> </div> </div> </div>\u200b<br /> <br /> So, on top of this, let's add Gnosis back into the game as a static value, rated between 1 and 5. This is used for shifting sideways, interacting with spirits, activating Gifts, and so on. For each Rage dice you have in your pool at a given time, you lose access to one point of Gnosis. This means that the higher your Rage gets, the harder it is to access the mystical aspects of being a werewolf. If your Rage dice pool raises to a point where a character no longer have access to Gnosis, that character is considered to have lost the connection to Gaia. When this happens, the character must add all their Rage dice to every dice pool. The urgency to achieve results means that the character pays no heed to the damage they do in the process. <br /> <br /> The end result is destructive and damaging, but possibly fun to play. You really want to get your Rage down in this situation, as, unless you do so, you are going to meet a bad end fairly quickly.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/317-james_willoughby","name":"James_Willoughby","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar317_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"297"}]},"thankedby":[]}]}